  "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/c4531d05ef2634d04e590b099ba5ec0269c9c00f12dce82442043cad4377db1a.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{-x}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x+3)$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{-x} & \\,\u27f9\\,x=-{y}^{2} \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) & \\,\u27f9\\,x=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2} & =2y-3 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y-3 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)(y-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100385/assets/3187c1520326caf6d009cacaf8b13bfc58a3e0eadbbdfd7aab01fb0d4154f476.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2y-3\\le x\\le -{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y-3}^{-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
